{"seeAlsoSections":[{"title":"Related Documentation","identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Accessibility"],"anchor":"Related-Documentation"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Overview","anchor":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The default Empty\/Loading\/Error placeholders’ text — “Nothing Here,” “Loading,” “Retry,” “Something Went Wrong,” “Connection Issue” — comes from a String Catalog, ","type":"text"},{"code":"Resources\/Localizable.xcstrings","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", resolved from ScreenStates’ own bundle (","type":"text"},{"code":"Bundle.module","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") rather than your app’s. That’s deliberate: a custom title your app passes in (see below) is never run back through localization, and a language you add to ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"your own"}]},{"text":" app’s catalog has no effect on these five strings — they only ever resolve against ScreenStates’ bundle. English, Spanish, French, and Russian are built in today.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Adding a language to ScreenStates","anchor":"Adding-a-language-to-ScreenStates"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"This is a change to the package itself, not something a consuming app can do on its own — it needs a PR. Open ","type":"text"},{"code":"Resources\/Localizable.xcstrings","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and add a ","type":"text"},{"code":"localizations","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" entry for the new language code under each of the five string keys:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"json","code":["\"Retry\" : {","  \"comment\" : \"Title of the button that retries loading after an error.\",","  \"localizations\" : {","    \"en\" : { \"stringUnit\" : { \"state\" : \"translated\", \"value\" : \"Retry\" } },","    \"de\" : { \"stringUnit\" : { \"state\" : \"translated\", \"value\" : \"Wiederholen\" } }","  }","}"],"type":"codeListing"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Every one of the five keys needs an entry for every language — a string present in English but missing a translation elsewhere fails CI (see below). ","type":"text"},{"code":"ScreenStatesStrings.swift","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"‘s doc comments list all five keys and their English text if you’re starting from scratch.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Overriding text per-app","anchor":"Overriding-text-per-app"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"You don’t need a new language, or a PR, to show different text than the built-in default — two options, depending on how much you want to replace:","type":"text"}]},{"items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":"ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView(title:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" takes a "},{"code":"title","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" directly, bypassing "},{"code":"Localizable.xcstrings","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" entirely for that one string. Pass your own localized string (from your own app’s String Catalog, "},{"code":"NSLocalizedString","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", whatever) instead of the default:"}]},{"type":"codeListing","code":["ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView(title: String(localized: \"No Results\"))"],"syntax":"swift"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":"ScreenStateDefaultErrorView","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" has no equivalent for its title or Retry button text today — only the error’s own message is customizable, via "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDisplayableError","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"For anything else — the Error placeholder’s title, the Retry button’s text, or any text at all in a shape the built-in views don’t expose a parameter for — pass a fully custom placeholder instead of the default one, via "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateView","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"type":"text","text":"’s "},{"code":"empty","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"loading","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"error","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" builder closures (see "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"text":") or ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateViewController","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":"‘s equivalent. A custom placeholder can still reuse ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState\/accessibilityAnnouncement","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" so VoiceOver announces transitions with the same wording your custom text uses, instead of the default’s.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"type":"unorderedList"}],"kind":"content"}],"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"major":0,"minor":3},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"How the default placeholders’ built-in text is localized, how to add a language, and how to show different text instead without touching ScreenStates itself."}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Localization","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}],"roleHeading":"Article","role":"article","title":"Localization"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates"]]},"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/screenstates\/localization"]}],"sections":[],"kind":"article","references":{"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/ScreenStateDisplayableError":{"title":"ScreenStateDisplayableError","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDisplayableError","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"ScreenStateDisplayableError","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/screenstatedisplayableerror","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"ScreenStateDisplayableError","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"text":"Lets an error type supply its own user-facing message, distinct from","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"localizedDescription","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" — which is often a debugging string (or, for a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"plain ","type":"text"},{"code":"Error","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" with no ","type":"text"},{"code":"CustomStringConvertible","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"LocalizedError","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"conformance, just the type name) rather than something to put in front","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"of the person using the app.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic"},"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","title":"Getting Started with SwiftUI","role":"article","kind":"article","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/gettingstartedwithswiftui","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Drive a SwiftUI screen’s four states with "},{"type":"reference